# Line endings are DECLARED, because this repository makes byte-exact assertions.
#
# WHAT THIS FIXES, measured 31 Aug 2026.
#
# There was no .gitattributes, so every checkout used whatever the contributor's
# git happened to be configured for. On Windows the default is
# core.autocrlf=true, which rewrites LF to CRLF on checkout. Simulating that
# conversion against the committed blobs:
#
#   sky.v1.json          recorded 128324   CRLF checkout 134041   +5717
#   sightings.v1.json    recorded 243228   CRLF checkout 256004  +12776
#   zones.v1.json        recorded  16282   CRLF checkout  16742    +460
#   items.v1.json        recorded   9797   CRLF checkout  10084    +287
#
# All four byte assertions in scripts/check.py fail, against byte-for-byte
# CORRECT content, and check.py then says "Do not commit until these are fixed."
#
# THE SIGNATURE IS THE WORST PART: the HASHES all stay green, because the check
# parses the JSON before re-serialising it and json.load discards whitespace. So
# it presents as four bytes-only failures with every hash clean, which reads like
# data corruption and is line endings. That is an hour of looking in the wrong
# place.
#
# It reaches further than those four. scripts/stamp.py fingerprints the raw bytes
# of every build input, so on a CRLF checkout the fingerprint of a correct tree
# does not match the committed stamp and check.py reports "public/ is stale" on a
# clone that has never been touched.
#
# Not an outage: .github/workflows/survey-refresh.yml runs on ubuntu-latest, so
# CI and the published site were never affected. This is a trap on contributors'
# machines, and both of ours are Windows.
#
# This is the checkout half of the same fault as scripts/stamp.py writing CRLF.
# That fixed the WRITE side; nothing declared the CHECKOUT side. A repository
# that has begun asserting byte counts has to declare its line endings, and the
# assertions were added without the declaration.

* text=auto eol=lf

# AND THE TRAP INSIDE THE FIX, which is why these lines exist.
#
# Three tracked text files carry CRLF *in the repository*, and two of them are
# the served application bundles — foreign artefacts whose sha1/sha256 is part of
# their own filename and is verified by scripts/check.py:
#
#   public/app/eqls-lockouts.16d4edad.html   3308 CRLF
#   public/app/sky-ledger.dad68d2b.html      2114 CRLF
#
# Normalising those would change their bytes, change their hash, and leave the
# served URL describing a file that no longer exists. `-text` keeps them exactly
# as the repository that built them produced them, which is the whole point of
# copying them in under a content hash.
public/app/* -text

# Hashed media, same reasoning: _build/media.py names each file after a sha1 of
# its own bytes.
_media/* -text
public/assets/media/* -text

# Belt and braces for anything git might mis-detect as text.
*.png  -text
*.jpg  -text
*.jpeg -text
*.webp -text
*.ico  -text
*.mp4  -text
*.webm -text
*.woff  -text
*.woff2 -text
*.ttf  -text
*.otf  -text
*.s3d  -text
*.zip  -text
